<item>
  <title>Utility and its function</title>
  <dc:creator>Matthew Munoz</dc:creator>
  <link>https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/utility/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>A quick little demonstration of utility functions</p>
<p><img src="https://your-website-url.example.com/posts/utility/thumbnail.jpg" class="img-fluid"></p>
<p>We are all well aware of utility functions. So aware that you may question why something like this is even necessary. Many would argue that it is not. But this is primarily to serve as a refresh to myself.</p>
<p>There are some notational things that do require some explanation prior to this refresh, so let’s brush up! ## Here we go</p>
<p>Well, let’s get a little bit abstract. Suppose there are two good, x and y that exist on the set of mutually exclusive alternatives. That is to say x, y represent two choices that you can not choose at once. Perhaps you want to go to sleep or go on a run or change your oil or etc. How could you do those simultaneously? The point being is that you can only pick one or the other. This is notationally equivalent to writing <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x,y%20%5Cin%20X%20%20"> Now we can introduce our so-called (weak) preference relation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim">. This is a “binary relation on the set of alternatives.” For our purposes, this just means that you can compare each element in the set. If x is weakly prefered to y (x <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> y), we can say that “x is at least as good as y.” Awesome.</p>
<p>The one thing about economics that we assume above all else is rationality. So we are going to assume that individual preferences are rational. To assume our assumption we must… make two assumptions about <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> : completeness and transitivity.</p>
<ol type="i">
<li>Completeness: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cforall%20x,y%20%5Cin%20X"> we have that x <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> y or that y <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> x or both</li>
<li>Transitivity: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cforall%20x,y,z%20%5Cin%20X"> we have that if x <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> y and y <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> z, then x <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> z</li>
</ol>
<p>This is fairly cool, I would say. Our consumer (by property of completeness) has a ranking of elements within the set, and (by property of transitivity) the ranking is consistent.</p>
<p>This is kind of a strong assumption, however. Imagine you, yourself were in a situation where… let’s say you are at Home Depot picking out a color for your kitchen. There are so many colors, so many choices! How can you look at them all and establish a ranking? Ah! But our individual consumer would have no problem. They would be able to walk into Home Depot, look at all the color options, rank them all, and have those rankings be consistent. That is far, far easier said than done.</p>
<p>Again, aside, etc. That should be the bare minimum necessary to go over utility functions.</p>
<section id="utility-functions" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="utility-functions">Utility functions!</h2>
<p>A utility function is something a bit abstract, but not to hard to wrap your mind around. The utility function maps each element from the set of alternatives onto the set of real numbers. This allows us to rank othe elements in accordance to the individuals preference.</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?u:X%20%5Cmapsto%20%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D"> is a function representing preference relation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> if for all x, y <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cin"> X <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x%20%5Csuccsim%20y%20%5Ciff%20u(x)%20%5Cge%20u(y)"> As you will soon come to know, a utility function that represents a preference relation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim"> is not unique. For any strictly increasing function <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f:%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%20%5Cmapsto%20%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D">, v(x) = f(u(x)). As long as the ranking of the alternatives is preserved, the utility function is valid!</p>
