<p>Yes, if you are wondering, I just wanted to show that R is capable of making function factories. That was the primary motivation in writing this. Putting that aside, let’s prove what that code is showing.</p>
</section>
<section id="proof" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="proof">Proof</h2>
<p>if <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f:%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D%20%5Cmapsto%20%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D">, and u:X <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmapsto%20%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D"> is a utility function representing preference relation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim">, then the function v:X <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cmapsto%20%20%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D"> defined by v(x) = f(u(x)) is also a utility function representing preference relation <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Csuccsim">.</p>
<p>We can prove this directly. Assume that f is a strictly increasing function and u(x) is a utility function representing preference relation.</p>
<p>By definition of strictly increasing, we know that for any <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x_2%20%3E%20x_1"> that <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?f(x_2)%20%3E%20f(x_1)"></p>
<p>By definition of a utility function representing preference relation, we know that <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?x%20%5Csuccsim%20y%20%5Ciff%20u(x)%20%5Cge%20u(y)">.</p>
<p>Observe: Consider the utility function u (by our assumption) <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x%20%5Csuccsim%20y%20%5Ciff%20u(x)%20%5Cge%20u(y)"> We define v(x) as f(u(x)). And since f is a strictly increasing function denoted by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x_2%20%3E%20x_1%20%5Ciff%20f(x_2)%20%3E%20f(x_1)%20"> which by contrapositive is logically equivalent to <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x_1%20%5Cge%20x_2%20%5Ciff%20f(x_1)%20%5Cge%20f(x_2)"> Since in our utility function we have u(x) <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cge"> u(y), we can then say that f(u(x)) <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cge"> f(u(y)), which is equivalent to: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20u(x)%20%5Cge%20u(y)%20%5Ciff%20v(x)%20%5Cge%20v(y)"> By definition of v(x) = f(u(x))! Thus we can say <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x%20%5Csuccsim%20y%20%5Ciff%20u(x)%20%5Cge%20u(y)%20%5Ciff%20v(x)%20%5Cge%20v(y)%20"> which means <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%20x%20%5Csuccsim%20y%20%5Ciff%20v(x)%20%5Cge%20v(y)%20"> Thus v is a utility function representing preference relation! fadadsf</p>


